   "body": "## 1. Operational Cost Baseline for the Region\n\nThis profile covers India's road freight and port sector, drawn from a policy commentary by the International Council on Clean Transportation (ICCT), dated September 15, 2025, and a programme page of WRI India, a research institute. The pages state no industrial electricity tariff, diesel price per litre, driver or warehouse labour rate, port handling charge or haulage rate, so a unit cost baseline cannot be given and none is invented.\n\nThey do give the weight of logistics in the economy. ICCT writes that «logistics costs, at about 13% of GDP, far exceed the global average.» The global average is not given a figure, so the size of the gap is not stated. WRI India adds that «Logistics costs remain high, with companies spending 10–15% of their expenses on transporting goods to and from factories.» On fuel, ICCT notes that «With 70% of freight moving by road and fuel making up a large portion of costs, electrifying trucks is both an environmental and economic imperative.» WRI India sizes the fleet and its fuel use: «comprising 6.3 million trucks and handling 70% of domestic freight», «consuming 838 billion liters of diesel annually and emitting 120 million tCO₂e».\n\nContext for the emissions side: «the sector contributes almost 14% of the country’s CO₂ emissions, with about 90% coming from road transport.» and, among trucks, «medium and heavy-duty trucks responsible for 44% of that share—despite comprising just 3% of vehicles on the road».\n\n## 2. Feasibility of Specific Operational Choices\n\nElectric trucks: «India has only 869 electric medium- and heavy-duty freight vehicles», against the fleet of 6.3 million trucks quoted in section 1. WRI India gives the reason: «The high upfront cost of electric trucks, coupled with limited charging infrastructure, remains a significant roadblock.» It adds that «Additional barriers, including a fragmented industry, the absence of standardized contracts, and a lack of strong regulatory mandates, further slow progress.» Direction exists on the policy side: «In July 2025, the Ministry of Heavy Industries released guidelines for electric trucks under the PM e-DRIVE scheme», and the e-FAST India platform, on which WRI India is lead partner, reports «securing commitments for 7,750 e-trucks at the Clean Energy Ministerial.», with «Facilitating early e-truck pilots in Gujarat, Rajasthan, and Maharashtra». *Analytical inference:* resting on these sentences, the position is restricted by cost and charging rather than barred by any rule the pages state, and the 7,750 trucks committed are many times the 869 on the road, so the first constraint on an electric truck option for a forwarder's Indian lanes is availability and charging, and the pages give no price or range for a truck to cost it.\n\nPorts: no page gives a permit verdict for on-site solar or equipment at a port or warehouse. The port sector is covered by targets in section 7 rather than by rules a user can be held to.\n\n## 7. Pending Changes That Shift the Calculus\n\nFuel efficiency standards for vehicles are not yet final. ICCT reports that «The Bureau of Energy Efficiency (BEE) has proposed Phase 3 and 4 of the Corporate Average Fuel Efficiency (CAFE) norms for passenger cars, and they are currently under finalization.» and that «BEE has released draft fuel-efficiency regulations for light-, medium-, and heavy-duty vehicles». No date for finalisation is given. *Analytical inference:* resting on these sentences, a rule on average fuel use of new trucks would change which vehicles truck makers sell, and so the fleet a forwarder's Indian carriers can buy, but the draft text and thresholds are not in the pages.\n\nOn ports and shipping, ICCT describes the Maritime Amrit Kaal Vision 2047 as «expanding port capacity from 2,500 million metric tonnes per annum (MMTPA) today to more than 10,000 MMTPA, supported by world-class infrastructure, digital innovation, hydrogen and ammonia hubs, carbon-neutral operations, and shore power at all major ports by 2047.» Alongside it, «the Harit Sagar Green Port Guidelines provide a framework to shrink the environmental footprint of ports—targeting over 60% renewable energy use by 2030 and 90% by 2047, electrifying half of port equipment by 2030 and 90% by 2047, and reducing CO₂ intensity per ton of cargo handled by more than 70% by 2047.» These are targets set out in guidelines, and the pages do not say who is bound to meet them or what follows if they are missed. Against the current 2,500 MMTPA, the vision's more than 10,000 MMTPA is a fourfold increase in stated port capacity (*Analytical inference:* 10,000 divided by 2,500).\n\nFor the economy as a whole, ICCT places this in «commitment to achieving net-zero emissions by 2070».\n\n- July 2025: «In July 2025, the Ministry of Heavy Industries released guidelines for electric trucks under the PM e-DRIVE scheme» (already released)\n- 2030: «over 60% renewable energy use by 2030» and half of port equipment electrified, under the Harit Sagar Green Port Guidelines\n- 2047: «shore power at all major ports by 2047» and 90% of port equipment electrified, under the Maritime Amrit Kaal Vision 2047 and the Harit Sagar Green Port Guidelines\n- 2070: «commitment to achieving net-zero emissions by 2070»\n\n## 8. Sources\n\n- International Council on Clean Transportation, \"India's transport transformation: Three policies to shape the road ahead\": https://theicct.org/indias-transport-transformation-three-policies-to-shape-the-road-ahead-sep25/\n- WRI India, \"Accelerating India's Freight Decarbonization\": https://wri-india.org/initiatives/accelerating-indias-freight-decarbonization\n",
