   "body": "## 1. Operational Cost Baseline for the Region\n\nThis profile covers Australia, using the Australian Institute of Petroleum (AIP) daily diesel price page and the Clean Energy Regulator's Safeguard Mechanism page. The closest sourced anchor for a diesel cost baseline is the Terminal Gate Price (TGP) the page publishes; it is a wholesale terminal price, not a lane quote or a pump price. The page states how the figure is built: «Prices shown are the average Terminal Gate Price for unleaded petrol and diesel across each of these companies for the day.» and it uses «using information provided by BP Australia, Ampol, Viva Energy Australia and ExxonMobil.»\n\nFor Sydney, the price table gives: «Diesel (cents per litre, inclusive of GST) Location Friday, 28th August 2026 Monday, 31st August 2026 Tuesday, 1st September 2026 Wednesday, 2nd September 2026 Thursday, 3rd September 2026 Sydney 241.7 240.5 238.2 236.9 237.5» Sydney therefore fell from 241.7 to 237.5 cents per litre over the five days shown. In the other cities, Melbourne ran «Melbourne 242.3 241.2 238.6 237.2 237.4», Perth «Perth 235.1 233.8 231.1 229.5 230.0» and Darwin «Darwin 251.2 249.9 247.3 245.8 246.3». *Analytical inference:* resting on these rows, the cheapest city on the last day shown is Perth at 230.0 and the dearest is Darwin at 246.3, a spread of 16.3 cents per litre, and every city is lower on the last day than on the first.\n\nThe pages state no industrial electricity tariff, labour rate, port handling charge or haulage rate for Australia, and the diesel figures are wholesale terminal prices including GST, not the retail pump price the page sends readers elsewhere for. No trend beyond these five days is given, so the direction of the price over a longer period is unknown.\n\n## 2. Feasibility of Specific Operational Choices\n\nNo captured page states a permit verdict (possible, restricted or prohibited) for on-site solar, battery storage, equipment import or in-region sourcing in Australia, so none is given. What the captured pages do establish is a reporting and emissions-baseline regime that large logistics sites can fall under, described in section 7, and a state of the national emissions debate in the same section.\n\n## 7. Pending Changes That Shift the Calculus\n\nThe Safeguard Mechanism is, in the regulator's words, «The Safeguard Mechanism is Australia's principal policy for reducing greenhouse gas emissions from the country’s largest industrial facilities.» It is tied to national targets: «It aligns with the Australian Government’s emissions reduction targets of 43% below 2005 levels by 2030 and net zero by 2050.» The regulator describes who it reaches: «The Safeguard Mechanism applies to responsible emitters of facilities that emit more than 100,000 tonnes of carbon dioxide equivalent (tCO 2 -e) covered emissions a year (or other monitoring period ). This includes mining, oil and gas production, manufacturing, transport and waste facilities.» *Analytical inference:* resting on this sentence, transport and waste sites are inside the scheme's stated coverage, but only a facility above 100,000 tonnes a year is reached; whether any given freight site is above the line is not stated and needs the regulator's own assessment.\n\n*Legal Confirmation Required:* whether a given freight site is a responsible emitter of a facility above the 100,000 tonne line, and so carries the 1 April excess-emissions duty or the 31 August NGER registration duty, is a question for the regulator or counsel. The yearly cycle is stated: «Responsible emitters of facilities with net emissions that exceed their baseline must take action to manage the excess before the 1 April compliance deadline.» The ways to manage an excess are: «They can either: surrender Australian carbon credit units (ACCUs) or Safeguard Mechanism credit units (SMCs) obtain a flexibility measure from the Clean Energy Regulator.» For a site not yet registered, «Responsible emitters that are not already registered must apply for NGER registration by 31 August after meeting the safeguard threshold.» The scheme is not new and is changing: it is dated by «deposited before the Safeguard Mechanism began on 1 July 2016», «The Safeguard Mechanism was reformed in 2023.» and the regulator's page, which carries «Last updated 6 August 2026», adds that «The department will review the Safeguard Mechanism’s policy settings in 2026–27».\n\nOn national targets, the Climate Change Authority published on «18 Sep 2025 The Authority’s 2035 Targets Advice recommends an emissions reduction target of 62–70% from 2005 levels.» The page describes this as advice, and the captured pages do not say that it has been adopted as a binding target. *Analytical inference:* resting on this sentence, a stricter national target would be a pressure on schemes such as the Safeguard Mechanism over time, but no adopted target or date for adoption is stated. On 1 Sep 2026 the Authority also reported on the carbon credit scheme: «1 Sep 2026 Australian carbon credits: sound foundations, new demands Authority finds the carbon credit scheme is fundamentally sound, but needs to evolve to support Australia’s climate goals.»\n\n- 1 July 2016: the Safeguard Mechanism began («deposited before the Safeguard Mechanism began on 1 July 2016»)\n- 2023: «The Safeguard Mechanism was reformed in 2023.»\n- 18 Sep 2025: Climate Change Authority 2035 Targets Advice published, recommending a target of 62-70% from 2005 levels\n- 3rd September 2026: latest diesel terminal gate price day captured in this profile\n- 2026-27: «The department will review the Safeguard Mechanism’s policy settings in 2026–27»\n\n## 8. Sources\n\n- Australian Institute of Petroleum, Terminal Gate Prices (prepared by ORIMA Research on behalf of the Institute): https://www.aip.com.au/pricing/terminal-gate-prices\n- Clean Energy Regulator, The Safeguard Mechanism: https://www.cleanenergyregulator.gov.au/NGER/The-safeguard-mechanism\n- Climate Change Authority, home page and 2035 Targets Advice: https://climatechangeauthority.gov.au/\n",
