   "body": "## 1. Operational Cost Baseline for the Region\n\nThis profile covers Brazil, drawing on the Ministry of Transportation's own portal, a clean-trucking programme's article on Brazil dated November 03, 2025, and a US trade agency note on Brazilian rail dated 09/03/2025. The Ministry describes the stakes in its own words: «Salvar vidas e ainda proteger a carga que representa cerca de 50% do PIB do país!» No page states a diesel price, electricity tariff, labour rate, port handling charge or haulage rate, so no unit cost baseline can be given and none is invented.\n\nWhat the sources do put numbers on is road investment, which sets where road capacity improves. The Ministry reports «Ministério dos Transportes destina R$ 1,45 bilhão para melhorias no Trecho do Meio da BR-319/AM Recursos contemplam intervenções em 339 quilômetros da rodovia, divididos em quatro lotes», «Com investimento de R$ 407,8 milhões, duplicação da BR-080 avança entre Taguatinga e Brazlândia Durante visita técnica, ministro dos Transportes George Santoro acompanhou o andamento das intervenções, que já alcançam cerca de 20% de execução» and «Com investimento de R$ 229,8 milhões, a obra nas BRs 235/407, entregue pelo ministro dos Transportes, George Santoro, vai reorganizar o tráfego entre Bahia e Pernambuco e tem conclusão prevista para 2026» Its concession news adds: «Consórcio 116 Sertões vence certame da BR-116/324/BA/PE com aporte de R$ 8,53 bilhões em infraestrutura» *Analytical inference:* ordered by size the four sums run 229,8 million (BRs 235/407), 407,8 million (BR-080), 1,45 billion (BR-319/AM) and 8,53 billion (BR-116/324), so the BR-116/324 concession is by far the largest; they are different instruments (a works contract, an allocation of funds, a concession bid), so they are not like-for-like, and the pages give no network-wide total or earlier-year figure to read any of them against.\n\nOn running cost, one vendor-side figure exists. «Compared with diesel-powered vehicles, electric vehicles deliver approximately 80% lower operating costs, 80% to over 100% higher energy efficiency», said by Gustavo Tannure, chief executive of the electromobility company EZVolt, as a company statement and not a regulator's finding. The statement gives no diesel price, vehicle model or mileage to measure it against, and the pages offer no neutral check on it.\n\nOn emissions: «the transportation sector is responsible for 53.3% of the energy sector’s GHG emissions in Brazil» The same article frames the national direction «as the country works to reach net-zero by 2050».\n\n## 2. Feasibility of Specific Operational Choices\n\nElectric trucks and vans: possible, and early. «the first 100% electric truck designed and built in Brazil», and «In all, more than 1,380 commercial electric vehicles were sold in the country between January and July 2025, including vans, buses, and trucks according to the ABVE.» The sources split that figure by type only as vans, buses and trucks, so the number of trucks alone is not stated. For buses, which are further along: «Brazil saw a 141 percent increase in new zero-emission bus sales in the first half of 2025.» The city of São Paulo drives this: «The city has banned the purchase of new diesel buses, and is aiming to have a 100% zero-emission fleet by 2038» *Analytical inference:* resting on these sentences, the evidence for electrified fleets in Brazil sits in urban buses, and the pages give no equivalent adoption figure for heavy-duty freight trucks.\n\nRail: the Ministry's model for idle lines is open to private operators. «This model permits private operators to assume control of underutilized railway segments», and for the first offer, «The first section to be offered is the Minas-Rio railway corridor (Central-Atlantic Railway). The selection process will begin between September and October 2025.» The source note is dated 09/03/2025, and the captured pages do not say whether that selection began, so its status is unknown. The proposal for idle lines is stated as follows: «The proposal is to reactivate these sections through new concession models with contracts of up to 99 years and free tariffs.» (the page does not define the term \"free tariffs\").\n\nCharging and platforms: «e-FAST Brasil is coordinated by WRI Brasil», and was «Launched in July of 2025». «national government recently announced the creation of an electromobility secretary under the Ministry of Mines and Energy»; the pages give no scope or start date for it.\n\n## 5. Competitive Positioning in the Region\n\nThree operators appear with a footprint. «EZVolt, the largest electromobility company in Brazil, services roughly 75% of the e-bus fleets from São Paulo city.» A photo caption in the same article reads «Zero-emission DHL truck charges in São Paulo.» and the caption names a carrier operating a zero-emission truck, though the article gives no fleet size, lane or price. On rail, «Currently, the Central Atlantic Railway is operated by VLI Logistica, which has just renewed its concession rights until 2056» *Analytical inference:* resting on these sentences, an established private operator already holds a long concession on one corridor, so any forwarder using that line deals with it rather than with the Ministry, and no tariff is stated.\n\n## 7. Pending Changes That Shift the Calculus\n\nHeavy-duty vehicle emissions: «In 2024, Brazil launched the MOVER program (Programa Nacional de Mobilidade Verde e Inovação), which is the first of its kind to regulate emissions from the heavy-duty transportation sector (with specific regulations expected for 2027/8).» «It offers significant incentives for innovation, while also creating new requirements for vehicle energy efficiency and emissions.» *Analytical inference:* resting on these sentences, rules on energy efficiency and emissions for heavy vehicles are expected after a delay of years from the 2024 launch, so the diesel truck fleet a forwarder's Brazilian carriers buy from then on may change; the text of the rules is not in the pages.\n\nTargets set by others: «The city has banned the purchase of new diesel buses, and is aiming to have a 100% zero-emission fleet by 2038», and «The Global MoU, co-led by CALSTART/Drive to Zero and Colombia, aims to support countries in reaching 100% new zero-emission truck and bus sales by 2040.»\n\nRoads: «Com investimento de R$ 229,8 milhões, a obra nas BRs 235/407, entregue pelo ministro dos Transportes, George Santoro, vai reorganizar o tráfego entre Bahia e Pernambuco e tem conclusão prevista para 2026»\n\nWhat changes on the dated events (*Analytical inference:* each resting on the sentence quoted above for that date): in 2026 the BRs 235/407 works are due to finish, and the Ministry says they will reorganise traffic between Bahia and Pernambuco, so the routing of road legs between those states may change; by 2038 São Paulo's city bus fleet is meant to be fully zero-emission, which affects urban charging demand and is not a freight rule; by 2040 the Global MoU aims at all new truck and bus sales being zero-emission, which would change the vehicles carriers can buy, but it is an aim of the signatories, not a rule in Brazil.\n\n- 2025: «Launched in July of 2025» (e-FAST Brasil platform)\n- 2025: «The first section to be offered is the Minas-Rio railway corridor (Central-Atlantic Railway). The selection process will begin between September and October 2025.»\n- 2026: «Com investimento de R$ 229,8 milhões, a obra nas BRs 235/407, entregue pelo ministro dos Transportes, George Santoro, vai reorganizar o tráfego entre Bahia e Pernambuco e tem conclusão prevista para 2026»\n- 2027: «In 2024, Brazil launched the MOVER program (Programa Nacional de Mobilidade Verde e Inovação), which is the first of its kind to regulate emissions from the heavy-duty transportation sector (with specific regulations expected for 2027/8).»\n- 2038: «The city has banned the purchase of new diesel buses, and is aiming to have a 100% zero-emission fleet by 2038»\n- 2040: «The Global MoU, co-led by CALSTART/Drive to Zero and Colombia, aims to support countries in reaching 100% new zero-emission truck and bus sales by 2040.»\n- 2056: «Currently, the Central Atlantic Railway is operated by VLI Logistica, which has just renewed its concession rights until 2056»\n\n## 8. Sources\n\n- Ministério dos Transportes, official portal (news items): https://www.gov.br/transportes/pt-br\n- Ministério dos Transportes, sustainability page: https://www.gov.br/transportes/pt-br/assuntos/sustentabilidade\n- Drive to Zero, \"Charging Up Change: Brazil Accelerating its Drive to Zero\": https://globaldrivetozero.org/2025/11/03/charging-up-change-brazil-accelerating-its-drive-to-zero/\n- US International Trade Administration, \"Brazil Rail Transportation\": https://www.trade.gov/market-intelligence/brazil-rail-transportation\n",
