   "body": "## 1. Operational Cost Baseline for the Region\n\nThis profile covers the European Union and rests on one article: Eurostat's Electricity price statistics. The stored title of this item names IEA large-industrial-customer price data; the pages held are Eurostat only and no IEA page was captured, so this profile does not cover IEA data. Electricity is a running cost for warehouses, cold stores and charging sites, so the EU price for non-household consumers (businesses, as distinct from households) is the baseline an EU facility's power bill is read against. The article states its date basis: «Data extracted in April 2026». It also states what the prices include: «Note that the prices presented in this article include taxes, levies and VAT for household consumers, but exclude refundable taxes and levies for non-household consumers.»\n\nThe headline: «Non-household electricity prices in the EU were highest in Ireland (€0.2552 per kWh) and lowest in Finland (€0.0748 per kWh) in the second half of 2025.» The article gives the EU average: «The EU average price in the second half of 2025 was €0.1837 per kWh.» For comparison, the same article puts the household average at «The EU average price in the second half of 2025 – a weighted average using the most recent (2024) consumption data for electricity by household consumers – was €0.2896 per kWh.» The business average is the lower of the two; the two figures are built on different tax treatment, as the article's note above states, so they are not like-for-like.\n\nThe countries around the average: «highest in Ireland (€0.2552 per kWh), Cyprus (€0.2429 per kWh) and Germany(€0.2264 per kWh). The lowest prices were observed in Finland (€0.0748 per kWh) and Sweden (€0.0970 per kWh).» The article lists the drivers of the price: «The price of energy in the EU depends on a range of different supply and demand conditions, including the geopolitical situation, the national energy mix, import diversification, network costs, environmental protection costs, severe weather conditions, or levels of excise and taxation.»\n\nTrend, with the earlier points for comparison: «peaked at €0.2151 per kWh in the first half of 2023» before «In the second half of 2024, the price rose by 2.9% to €0.1941 per kWh. It then eased again, falling to €0.1903 per kWh in the first half of 2025 and to €0.1837 per kWh in the second half of 2025, a further 3.5% decline.» «and further decreased in the second half to €0.1511, which is the lowest since the beginning of 2022.» That €0.1511 is the price excluding taxes; the 0.2151, 0.1941 and 0.1837 figures are total prices including non-recoverable taxes, so the two series are not set side by side. The article puts the tax share at «In the first half of 2025, the proportion of taxes increased to 17.5%, and to 17.7% in the second half.» *Analytical inference:* resting on these figures, the second-half 2025 price of 0.1837 is below the 0.2151 peak and below the 0.1941 of a year earlier, while the share of non-recoverable taxes in the price is edging up. The article's summary reads: «Electricity prices for non-household consumers in the EU fell by 5.4% in the second half of 2025 compared to the same period in 2024 and by 3.5% from the first half of 2025.»\n\nThe article gives no diesel price, labour rate, port handling charge or haulage rate; this profile is an electricity cost baseline only.\n\n## 2. Feasibility of Specific Operational Choices\n\nNo page in the captured material gives a permit verdict (possible, restricted or prohibited) for on-site solar, battery storage, equipment import or in-region sourcing in any EU country, so none is given here. The price spread in section 4 is the only input to such a decision that the sources hold.\n\n## 4. Cross-Regional Strategic Implications\n\nThe article's own range gives the siting signal. The article states: «Non-household electricity prices in the EU were highest in Ireland (€0.2552 per kWh) and lowest in Finland (€0.0748 per kWh) in the second half of 2025.» *Analytical inference:* resting on those two figures, a site in Ireland pays about 3.4 times the Finnish rate (0.2552 divided by 0.0748), so for a power-heavy activity such as cold storage or charging the country of the facility moves the electricity bill far more than the movement of the EU average over two years. The article does not tie any price to freight or logistics sites, and it does not say whether a given site falls in the consumption band it uses, so the size of the effect on a particular site is not stated.\n\n## 7. Pending Changes That Shift the Calculus\n\nThe only dated forward step in the material is the article's own update schedule: «Planned article update: 30 October 2026». That date is now past. The article was extracted in April 2026 and the captured pages do not hold whether the October update was published or what it showed, so every figure above is that of the April 2026 extraction. *Analytical inference:* resting on the update notice, the update was the point at which the trend in section 1 would be confirmed or reversed; the pages state no forecast of direction.\n\n- April 2026: «Data extracted in April 2026»\n- 30 October 2026: «Planned article update: 30 October 2026»\n\n## 8. Sources\n\n- Eurostat, Statistics Explained, \"Electricity price statistics\": https://ec.europa.eu/eurostat/statistics-explained/index.php?title=Electricity_price_statistics\n",
