   "body": "## 1. Operational Cost Baseline for the Region\n\nThis profile covers the European Union, using two pages of the European Commission's Directorate-General for Energy: its Energy prices and costs in Europe page (summarising the sixth report, «was published in February 2025.») and its Market analysis page (quarterly gas and electricity reports, latest Q2 2025 in the captured material). The captured material stops at Q2 2025, so nothing later is stated here, and every figure below is a Q2 2025 or earlier reading.\n\nGas, wholesale and retail: «Wholesale prices decreased by 24% and retail prices by 2% quarter-on-quarter. Year-on-year, wholesale and retail prices were still moderately higher, by 10% and 9%, respectively, as the start of the new declining trend has not yet fully offset prices increases in the previous 4 quarters.» Within that quarter, «retail prices steadily decreased in Q2-2025 from April (111.4 €/MWh) through May (109.1€/MWh) and June (108.7€/MWh) 2025». *Analytical inference:* resting on these readings, EU retail gas ended the second quarter at 108.7 €/MWh, below April's 111.4, which fits the Commission's quarterly retail decline of 2%.\n\nElectricity (a household price in capital cities, given for the trend and not as a business or warehouse price): «Retail electricity prices for households in EU capital cities rose marginally by 3% in Q2 2025 (to 246 €/MWh).» The Commission explains: «despite the energy component decreasing compared to last year’s quarter as both taxes and network costs increased.» It adds that «However, prices were much lower than in the first quarter of the year». The mix behind the price: «The share of renewables stayed stable at 52% in Q2 2025 (same as in Q2 2024)». The gas supply mix: «The share of LNG in overall EU gas supply further increased to 46%.» «The share of U.S. LNG increased to 58% from 44% in the same quarter of the previous year, a 14 percentage point increase year-on-year.»\n\nThe longer view from the price-and-cost page: «Wholesale prices have stabilised on both electricity and gas markets since the energy crisis of 2021-2023, although at a level higher than their historical averages.» «the drop in wholesale prices is yet to bring down retail energy prices , which are still higher for households and enterprises than before 2021.» On the spread across countries, «Retail prices reflected the increases in wholesale prices, but not uniformly (in the magnitude and the speed of the pass through) across EU countries.»\n\nThe pages give no diesel price, labour rate, port handling charge or haulage rate; this is an energy cost baseline only.\n\n## 2. Feasibility of Specific Operational Choices\n\nNo captured page states a permit verdict (possible, restricted or prohibited) for on-site solar, battery storage, equipment import or in-region sourcing in any EU country, so none is given; the supply-mix readings are in section 1.\n\n## 4. Cross-Regional Strategic Implications\n\nThe Commission compares the EU with its main trading partners: «industrial gas and electricity prices, while lower than during the crisis, are still 2-4 times higher than in the EU’s main trading partners, which threatens the long-term competitiveness of European industry.» «Household gas prices were almost twice as high in 2023 than before the crisis.» The cost of imported energy sits behind this: «energy import bill receded to €427 billion in 2024 (after reaching the peak of €604 billion in 2022), is still a significant drain on the European economy.» *Analytical inference:* resting on these sentences, industrial gas and electricity prices at an EU site are higher than in the EU's main trading partners, a gap the Commission names as a threat to the competitiveness of European industry; the pages do not say how this feeds through to freight volumes or to warehouse and cold-store rates.\n\nFor comparison on road electrification, the market page records on passenger cars: «This translates into a 23% EV share in the EU passenger car market, more than two times the market share registered in the United States (10%).» The scope is passenger cars; the pages give no equivalent figure for trucks or vans.\n\n## 7. Pending Changes That Shift the Calculus\n\nThe pages announce no dated regulation, consultation or infrastructure step. The one forward-looking statement for gas is that «the second quarter of 2025 witnessed the start of a new price decline trend», and for electricity that prices «However, prices were much lower than in the first quarter of the year». *Analytical inference:* resting on these statements, the Q2 2025 direction of EU energy prices was down from the first quarter, but no figure or date for later quarters is stated, and this material is more than a year old; the Commission's later quarterly reports are not in the captured pages.\n\n- February 2025: «was published in February 2025.»\n- Q2 2025: «the second quarter of 2025 witnessed the start of a new price decline trend»\n\n## 8. Sources\n\n- European Commission, DG Energy, Energy prices and costs in Europe: https://energy.ec.europa.eu/data-and-analysis/energy-prices-and-costs-europe_en\n- European Commission, DG Energy, Market analysis (quarterly gas and electricity reports): https://energy.ec.europa.eu/data-and-analysis/market-analysis_en\n",
