   "body": "## 1. Operational Cost Baseline for the Region\n\nThis profile covers Iowa, in the United States, and rests on Iowa Department of Transportation (Iowa DOT) planning pages, not on a price list. For a freight operator the question is where public freight money and rules are decided for a state that much freight only crosses. The Iowa DOT says so itself: «a significant amount of freight originates outside of Iowa and moves though the state to destinations in other states.» It lists what carries that freight: «transportation system of airports, highways, pipelines, railroads, and waterways play a major role in supporting the state’s economy.»\n\nThe captured pages state no diesel price, electricity tariff, labour rate, port handling charge or haulage rate for Iowa, so no cost baseline can be given and none is invented. The Iowa DOT's motor carrier navigation lists these services by name: «IFTA (International Fuel Tax Agreement) IRP (International Registration Plan) UCR (Unified Carrier Registration)». The pages list them as services and give no fee, so they are not a cost figure.\n\n## 2. Feasibility of Specific Operational Choices\n\nNo captured page states a permit verdict (possible, restricted or prohibited) for on-site solar, battery storage, equipment import or in-region sourcing in Iowa, so none is given. The pages describe the Freight Advisory Council in section 5.\n\n## 5. Competitive Positioning in the Region\n\nThe Freight Advisory Council is the advice channel the pages describe: «This group was created in 2012 as a forum to assist with understanding the complexities associated with freight movements through education, discussion, and review.» Its members, «Members representing the agriculture, energy, distribution, logistics, and multimodal transportation industries, as well as local and state government agencies, meet on a quarterly basis» Its listed duties include to «Assist in the development of the Iowa DOT’s State Freight Plan, including providing stakeholder input, reviewing draft content, and offering suggestions for improvement.» The Council's membership shows which operators sit at the table with the Iowa DOT. Road carriers: «Chief Commercial Officer, Ruan Transportation» and «Chief Operating Officer, Decker Truck Lines». Railroads: «Director of U.S. Government Affairs, CPKC Railroad», «Director of Public Affairs for IA, MN, and WI, Union Pacific Railroad» and «Chief Operating Officer, Iowa Interstate Railroad». A port body: «Vice-Chairman, Mid-America Port Commission». Shippers: «Manager of Factory Order Fulfillment, John Deere Des Moines Works» and «Assistant General Manager, Amazon DSM5». The latest meeting listed is «March 2026 Meeting Materials». *Analytical inference:* resting on these entries, the roster listed here includes two truck carriers, three railroads, a port commission and two shippers among its members; the pages give no fleet size, lane or price for any of them, and do not say how the Council's advice is weighed.\n\n## 7. Pending Changes That Shift the Calculus\n\nThe current State Freight Plan history is stated by the Iowa DOT: «The final State Freight Plan was presented for information to the Iowa Transportation Commission on May 10, 2022 and then forwarded to the Federal Highway Administration for approval. The plan was formally approved on August 18, 2022.» The plan «This plan is the second in the current series of freight plans that are now federally required to be updated every four years.» *Analytical inference:* resting on that sentence, four years from August 18, 2022 points to an update in 2026, which fits the draft whose comment period closed on April 9, 2026 (outcome not held).\n\nThe draft update was announced on a news item headed «Iowa DOT Invites Comments on the Draft State Freight Plan Tuesday, February 24, 2026», in which the Iowa DOT is «The Iowa Department of Transportation (DOT) is encouraging the public, freight industry and multimodal transportation representatives, and other interested stakeholders to review and provide comments regarding the draft State Freight Plan.» The item asks for comments «by April 9, 2026». That date is past; the captured pages do not say whether the plan was finalised after the comment period, so the status of the update is unknown and the 2022 plan is the last approved version shown.\n\nOther plans: «The 2025 Iowa State Rail Plan is intended to guide the Iowa Department of Transportation in its activities of promoting access to rail transportation, helping to improve the freight railroad transportation system, expanding passenger rail service, and promoting improved safety both on the rail system and where the rail system interacts with people and other transportation modes.» On the long-range plan, «state transportation plan was adopted in May 2022.» and «The plan is updated every five years because Iowa’s transportation system is ever-changing.» *Analytical inference:* resting on those sentences, the next long-range plan update falls around 2027, five years after May 2022. Federal oversight: «The FHWA monitors and approves all State Freight Plan updates to ensure compliance with current federal surface transportation laws.»\n\n- 2012: «This group was created in 2012 as a forum to assist with understanding the complexities associated with freight movements through education, discussion, and review.»\n- May 10, 2022: final State Freight Plan presented to the Iowa Transportation Commission\n- August 18, 2022: State Freight Plan formally approved\n- February 24, 2026: draft State Freight Plan opened for public comment\n- April 9, 2026: public comments were due on the draft State Freight Plan (date now past)\n\n## 8. Sources\n\n- Iowa DOT, Freight: https://iowadot.gov/transportation-development/systems-planning/areas-planning/freight\n- Iowa DOT, Freight Plan: https://iowadot.gov/transportation-development/systems-planning/areas-planning/transportation-plans/freight-plan\n- Iowa DOT, news item \"Iowa DOT Invites Comments on the Draft State Freight Plan\": https://iowadot.gov/news/2026-02-24/iowa-dot-invites-comments-draft-state-freight-plan\n- Iowa DOT, Freight Advisory Council: https://iowadot.gov/transportation-development/systems-planning/areas-planning/freight/freight-advisory-council\n- Iowa DOT, Rail Plan and State Transportation Plan: https://iowadot.gov/transportation-development/systems-planning/areas-planning/transportation-plans/rail-plan\n- Federal Highway Administration, State Freight Plans: https://ops.fhwa.dot.gov/freight/fpcb/toolkit/allplans.aspx\n",
