   "body": "## 1. Operational Cost Baseline for the Region\n\nThis item holds no sourced cost figure for Louisiana freight operations, so the cost anchor is a gap and not an estimate. The Louisiana Department of Transportation describes its freight plan this way: «The Louisiana State Freight Plan(SFP) provides the state with a blueprint for facilitating continued economic growth through a comprehensive, multimodal strategy for addressing freight transportation needs and moving goods efficiently and safely throughout the state.» The same page lists what the plan will do, including «Meet federal guidelines and support future planning, policies and investment in Louisiana». It states no rate, tariff or surcharge of any kind. The item's title also names a Commodity Flow Dashboard, which is not among the pages held, so no commodity flow figure is stated. The national page of current plans lists Louisiana: «Louisiana Louisiana Freight Plan».\n\n*Analytical inference:* the page says the plan will support future planning, policies and investment in Louisiana and states no published rate, so any effect on a forwarder's costs is not stated (resting on the \"planning, policies and investment\" sentence above). The sources held for this item name no competitor, no technology deployment and no research finding for Louisiana, so this profile serves the cost, regulatory and client lenses only.\n\n## 2. Feasibility of Specific Operational Choices\n\nThe captures state no verdict (possible, restricted or prohibited) on any specific operational choice in Louisiana, such as equipment, fuel or sourcing, so the feasibility question is a gap in the sources. The Louisiana page states the plan's purposes and no operating rule, and the federal notice that bears on the plan is set out under pending changes below.\n\n*Analytical inference:* resting on the Louisiana page, which describes a planning blueprint and not an operating rule, nothing in the held pages permits or restricts a particular operating choice for a forwarder in Louisiana.\n\n## 3. Cost Comparison Against Alternatives\n\n*No content for this section as of 2026-11-01: the sources held for this item compare no cost alternatives for Louisiana.*\n\n## 7. Pending Changes That Shift the Calculus\n\nThe federal guidance on state freight plans changed in 2026, and Louisiana's next update has to follow it. The notice states: «Unless otherwise stated in this Notice, this guidance is effective February 24, 2026.» It replaces older text: «This document updates and replaces the prior guidance on State Freight Plans and State Freight Advisory Committees, issued on January 12, 2023.» The update rhythm is the lever: the law cut the cycle, «reducing the update cycle for State Freight Plans from five years to four», and the notice repeats that «Plans must be updated at least once every four years.» The Louisiana page presents the «2024 Louisiana State Freight Plan» as its executive summary.\n\n*Legal Confirmation Required:* whether the National Highway Freight Program funding condition (a state may not obligate those funds once its four-year update cycle has expired unless its plan meets the existing requirements) and the duty to update the plan at least once every four years bear on a specific Louisiana project or contract is a question for counsel; the notice binds states and the sources name no duty on a forwarder. The notice states the funding condition as: «If a State's four-year update cycle has expired, then unless the State has developed its State Freight Plan consistent with the existing requirements, the State may not obligate NHFP funds».\n\n*Analytical inference:* resting on those sentences, Louisiana's next plan update falls under the February 24, 2026 guidance, and a plan that lapses would stop the state obligating National Highway Freight Program funds. The sources hold no date for the next Louisiana update and no statement of what it will change, so the date and the content are gaps; no further pending change for Louisiana is held.\n\n- February 24, 2026: DOT guidance on multimodal State Freight Plans took effect, replacing the guidance issued on January 12, 2023.\n- Four years: the longest interval the federal text allows between updates of a State Freight Plan, down from five years.\n\n## 8. Sources\n\n- Louisiana Department of Transportation, State Freight Plan page: https://latransportationplan.la.gov/freight-mobility-plan/\n- U.S. Department of Transportation, Federal Register notice of February 24, 2026, Guidance on Multimodal State Freight Plans and State Freight Advisory Committees: https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/full_text/text/2026/02/24/2026-03648.txt\n- Federal Highway Administration, Freight Professional Capacity Building Program, all current State Freight Plans: https://ops.fhwa.dot.gov/freight/fpcb/toolkit/allplans.aspx\n",
