   "body": "## 1. Operational Cost Baseline for the Region\n\nThis item has no single region: its sources are a United States report and an international statistics service, so the baseline below is for the United States and the international source is a pointer, not a number. The National Academies report on transportation puts the size of the workforce this way: «The 14.3 million transportation and transportation-related industry employees in the United States represent 10.2 percent of the entire U.S. workforce, a share that has remained steady since 2000.» On pay it says average wages and benefits in transportation are at levels «that have ranged from 15 to 20 percent less than the average for all occupations since 2005». Within that workforce the report singles out warehousing: «Together, these categories account for 18 percent of all transportation jobs, and both groups have grown sharply since 2010» The categories it names are warehousing and couriers and messengers.\n\nRail shows what a labour cost reset looks like: «The forced settlement raised average wages by 24 percent over 5 years» for railroad workers. The International Labour Organization's ILOSTAT page lists the indicator «Mean nominal hourly labour cost per employee by economic activity» for download, but the captured page holds no hourly figure for any country, so the hourly cost for any region is a gap here.\n\n*Analytical inference:* pay in United States transportation has sat below the all-occupation average for two decades, so a carrier or warehouse that competes for staff on pay starts from a lower base than other industries (resting on the 15 to 20 percent sentence above), while a single settlement can move one group's wages by a quarter in five years (resting on the 24 percent sentence). The sources name no competitor, no technology deployment and no research finding specific to a region, so this profile serves the cost, regulatory and client lenses only.\n\n## 2. Feasibility of Specific Operational Choices\n\nThe captures state no verdict (possible, restricted or prohibited) on a specific operational choice, so the feasibility analysis here is inference. The National Academies report says truckload drivers «now also work very long hours (up to and beyond the 60-hour-per-week limit on driving set in regulation)». It describes the daily pattern as «Long days (up to 11 hours driving within a total of 14 hours on duty)». On why drivers are scarce it says «challenges in recruiting and retaining long-distance truck drivers appear to reflect working conditions and compensation levels that many workers find unappealing».\n\n*Legal Confirmation Required:* the report cites a weekly driving limit set in regulation but does not give the rule itself, so what the limit is and whom it binds is a question for counsel or the regulator; the sources hold no rule text.\n\n*Analytical inference:* resting on those sentences, the report records drivers working up to and beyond the cited limit, so how far a carrier can lengthen driving days to cover a shortage is not settled by these sources, and the report ties hiring difficulty to working conditions and pay only as an appearance. The sources hold no verdict for any other country, so the feasibility of this choice elsewhere is a gap.\n\n## 7. Pending Changes That Shift the Calculus\n\nThe report names two forces that can move freight labour demand. On automation it says «the slow transition to higher levels of automation may significantly improve freight productivity and reduce the demands on human operators that lead to errors, but automation may significantly reduce employment in freight as well». On the energy transition it says jobs may shift: «overall employment in motor vehicle manufacturing and fossil fuel production for transportation may fall even as other manufacturing and blue-collar jobs associated with the energy transition expand». The report says «Trucking alone employs about 3.5 million drivers», so a change in driver demand reaches one of the largest occupations it names. No date is given for either shift, so the timing is a gap.\n\n*Analytical inference:* resting on the automation sentence, the labour question for road freight is two-sided, easing the demands on drivers while possibly cutting the number of jobs, and the report gives no schedule, so a pricing lead should treat it as a direction to watch and not a dated cost change.\n\n- late 2022: «Congress intervened in late 2022 to avoid a nationwide strike of railroad workers», and the forced settlement raised average wages by 24 percent over 5 years.\n- October 2023: «Labor strikes in automobile manufacturing at the time of this writing in October 2023 reflect worker concerns about lower compensation in EV and battery factories».\n\n## 8. Sources\n\n- National Academies of Sciences, Engineering, and Medicine, Critical Issues in Transportation for 2024 and Beyond, Workforce chapter: https://www.nationalacademies.org/read/27432/chapter/15\n- International Labour Organization, ILOSTAT, Statistics on labour costs: https://ilostat.ilo.org/topics/labour-costs\n- International Labour Organization, ILOSTAT, Data tools to find and download labour statistics: https://ilostat.ilo.org/data/\n- Bureau of Transportation Statistics, Latest Supply Chain and Freight Indicators: https://www.bts.gov/freight-indicators\n",
