   "body": "## 1. Operational Cost Baseline for the Region\n\nThe jurisdiction this brief covers is the State of Colorado, and its source is the Colorado Department of Transportation (CDOT). The sources hold no freight rate, toll, fuel price or labour cost for Colorado, so the cost baseline is a gap and not an estimate. The only cost document the page names is a «Cost Benefit Analysis available upon request», and its contents are not in the sources.\n\n*Analytical inference:* the Standard acts on which transport projects get planned and not on what freight costs today, so any effect on a forwarder's cost would arrive later through the road capacity that is or is not planned (resting on the planning content in the next section). The sources name no competitor, no technology deployment and no research finding, so this profile serves the regulatory, cost and client lenses only.\n\n## 2. Feasibility of Specific Operational Choices\n\nThe captures state no verdict (possible, restricted or prohibited) on a specific operational choice for a freight operator in Colorado, so the feasibility analysis here is inference. What they do state is how road projects are tested: «To determine compliance with the reduction levels, agencies must model their existing transportation networks and all future regionally significant capacity projects in their transportation planning documents». If an agency falls short, the page says they can «change the mix of projects in their transportation plans and/or use GHG Mitigation Measures», and it calls the measures «an important, but voluntary, component of the GHG Transportation Planning Standard».\n\n*Legal Confirmation Required:* whether the modelling duty and the reduction levels bear on a given freight project is a question for counsel; they bind CDOT and the planning organizations, and the sources name no duty on a forwarder.\n\n*Analytical inference:* resting on those sentences, regionally significant road capacity is modelled in the planning documents against the reduction levels, and a plan that misses them can be reshaped; the page does not say that construction waits on the model. An operator relying on a planned highway expansion should check that the project is still in the agency's plan. The sources do not say that any freight project has been changed or dropped, so that is a gap.\n\n## 4. Cross-Regional Strategic Implications\n\nThe rule is statewide but is applied region by region. It is one strategy within a different instrument, the state's climate plan: «Colorado's GHG Pollution Reduction Roadmap was updated in 2024», after the first Roadmap was «finalized in January 2021» according to the North Front Range planning organization. Under the Standard, «CDOT and the state’s five Metropolitan Planning Organizations (MPOs) are required to achieve individually set GHG reduction levels at four different time periods - 2025, 2030, 2040, and 2050». The Commission «has also voted via resolution to accept GHG Transportation Reports of DRCOG, NFRMPO, GVMPO, and PPACG, and PACOG», and the page says those reports show compliance with the reduction levels. The North Front Range organization's own page records that it adopted its first report in 2022: «In 2022, the NFRMPO adopted its first Greenhouse Gas (GHG) Transportation Report».\n\n*Legal Confirmation Required:* whether and how the reduction levels and the acceptance of reports bind a particular agency or project is a question for counsel; the sources name duties on CDOT and the planning organizations only.\n\n*Analytical inference:* resting on those sentences, each Colorado planning region carries its own reduction level and its own report, so freight routing through the Front Range and routing through another region face separate project screens, and the sources give no level for any region or any freight corridor, so a corridor-by-corridor comparison is a gap.\n\n## 7. Pending Changes That Shift the Calculus\n\nThe latest step is recent and the next level date is ahead. The page states: «CDOT's 2026 GHG Transportation Report was approved by the Transportation Commission on March 19, 2026 for CDOT's new FY2027-2036 10-Year Plan.» The first report is older: «CDOT's first GHG Report, approved on September 15, 2022». The reduction levels run to a schedule, «2025, 2030, 2040, and 2050», of which 2030 is the next one after the dates already passed. The rule itself was approved earlier: «On December 16, 2021, the Transportation Commission voted to approve a groundbreaking new rule», the GHG Transportation Planning Standard.\n\n*Analytical inference:* resting on the March 19, 2026 approval and the level schedule, the plan that now governs Colorado projects was tested under the Standard, and the next test point is the 2030 level, for which the sources give no figure, so the size of the 2030 reduction is a gap. The target years are levels to meet, not filing deadlines.\n\n- December 16, 2021: Transportation Commission approved the GHG Transportation Planning Standard.\n- September 15, 2022: CDOT's first GHG Report approved.\n- March 19, 2026: CDOT's 2026 GHG Transportation Report approved for the FY2027-2036 10-Year Plan.\n- 2030: next GHG reduction level in the Standard's schedule of 2025, 2030, 2040 and 2050.\n\n## 8. Sources\n\n- Colorado Department of Transportation, Greenhouse Gas (GHG) Program: https://codot.gov/programs/environmental/greenhousegas\n- North Front Range Metropolitan Planning Organization, Greenhouse Gas (GHG): https://nfrmpo.org/air-quality/ghg/\n",
