   "body": "## 1. Operational Cost Baseline for the Region\n\nFor scope, the statute says «transportation system is defined as all modes of transportation infrastructure owned and maintained by the North Carolina Department of Transportation, including roads, highways, rail, ferry, aviation, public transportation, and bicycle and pedestrian facilities.» The jurisdiction this brief covers is North Carolina, and its sources are the North Carolina Department of Transportation (NCDOT) and the North Carolina General Assembly. The sources hold no freight rate, toll, fuel price or labour cost for North Carolina, so the cost baseline is a gap and not an estimate. What they do hold is the state's spending plan for roads: the State Transportation Improvement Program, «which identifies transportation projects that will receive funding from 2026 to 2035», is large, covering «2,900 projects across North Carolina.» NCDOT's rail division says: «The North Carolina Department of Transportation's Rail Division has released its State Rail Plan for public comment.» The plan is described as the guide for public money in rail: «The State Rail Plan helps identify needs and guides public investments in North Carolina's freight and passenger rail network.» The plan «estimates levels of financial investment necessary to meet future railroad demands», but the captured release gives no amount.\n\n*Analytical inference:* resting on those sentences, a forwarder's North Carolina costs are shaped indirectly by which road and rail projects the state funds over 2026 to 2035, and the dollar size of the rail investment need is a gap because the release states none. The sources name no competitor, no technology deployment and no research finding, so this profile serves the cost, regulatory and client lenses only.\n\n## 2. Feasibility of Specific Operational Choices\n\nThe captures state no verdict (possible, restricted or prohibited) on a specific operational choice such as equipment or fuel in North Carolina, so the feasibility analysis here is inference. The one operating route the sources address is rail access: the State Rail Plan names two key initiatives that bear on freight movement, «Increased access to ports and inland freight facilities» and «Continued grade separation and crossing improvement projects to increase safety.»\n\n*Analytical inference:* resting on those sentences, the state treats rail access to ports and inland freight facilities as a key initiative, so moving cargo by rail to or from North Carolina ports is a route the state plans to widen; it is not stated as available today, and the sources give no date, location or funding for any such project, so the timing of that access is a gap.\n\n## 7. Pending Changes That Shift the Calculus\n\nThe road programme is set and the next one is already being built. NCDOT records that «In July 2025, the N.C. Board of Transportation adopted the 2026-2035» programme, that «The initial draft STIP was released in January 2025.», and that final federal sign-off came late in the year: «Federal Highway Administration in December 2025.» The project pipeline began earlier: «Projects were submitted for consideration from July to October 2023.» The page also lists «2028-2037 STIP Development», so a successor programme is in development. The rail plan has a recent date and a closed comment window: «Last updated 8:33 a.m. on Dec. 22, 2025», and «Public comment on the report with be open through Jan. 20, 2026.»\n\nThe legislature watches delivery. Before the Board approves a programme, the statute sets a lead time: «At least 30 days before it approves a Transportation Improvement Program». Delivery reports follow a fixed date: «The Department shall submit the report required under this subsection to the Joint Legislative Transportation Oversight Committee by March 1 of each year.» The reports cover «Bridge projects with a cost in excess of ten million dollars ($10,000,000).» and delays are explained: «the report shall include an indication and explanation for project stages that are delayed during the period covered by the report and the delay has been for more than one year». If a division has «three or more project stages delayed for more than one year», the statute says the Division Engineer must consult the committee.\n\n*Legal Confirmation Required:* whether the 30 day submission, the March 1 report and the consultation duty apply to a specific freight project is a question for counsel; the statute binds the Department and its Highway Divisions, and the sources name no duty on a forwarder.\n\n*Analytical inference:* resting on the March 1 date and the one-year delay test, a delayed bridge project costing over ten million dollars, or a delayed interstate, rural or urban highway project, in North Carolina becomes visible to legislators once a year, and the rail comment window closed on January 20, 2026 with no outcome in the sources, so what the final rail plan adopts is a gap. The 2028-2037 programme has no date in the sources.\n\n- January 2025: initial draft of the 2026-2035 STIP released.\n- July 2025: N.C. Board of Transportation adopted the 2026-2035 STIP.\n- December 2025: Federal Highway Administration gave final approval of the STIP.\n- December 22, 2025: NCDOT released the State Rail Plan for public comment.\n- January 20, 2026: public comment on the State Rail Plan closed.\n\n## 8. Sources\n\n- North Carolina Department of Transportation, About the 2026-2035 STIP: https://www.ncdot.gov/initiatives-policies/Transportation/stip/Pages/about.aspx\n- North Carolina Department of Transportation, NCDOT Releases State Rail Plan for Public Comment: https://www.ncdot.gov/news/press-releases/Pages/2025/2025-12-22-state-rail-plan-released-for-comment.aspx\n- North Carolina General Assembly, General Statutes Chapter 136, Transportation: https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/HTML/ByChapter/Chapter_136.html\n- North Carolina General Assembly, Joint Legislative Transportation Oversight Committee: https://www.ncleg.gov/Committees/CommitteeInfo/NonStanding/468\n",
