   "body": "## 1. Operational Cost Baseline for the Region\n\nThe jurisdiction this brief covers is not a single state: its sources describe world shipping chokepoints, so the region anchor is stated as an ambiguity and not narrowed to one country. The cost anchors are modelled and reported figures, not tariffs. A Nature Communications study, marked «Published: 24 November 2025», says: «We estimate the economic losses of chokepoint disruptions, due to delays, rerouting, insurance premiums and trade disruptions, to be USD10.7 billion per year, and an additional USD3.4 billion per year due to increased freight costs.» Those losses sit against a much larger exposure, since the expected value of trade disrupted is put at «be USD192 billion» annually. The study adds that «risks to the Suez Canal and Bab el-Mandeb Strait drive these losses.»\n\nUNCTAD's Review of Maritime Transport 2024 gives the operating cost side: «Longer routes have increased costs for fuel, wages, insurance and chartering while boosting emissions.» It puts one number on the carbon part: «For a 20,000-24,000 TEU vessel on the Far East-Europe route, CO2 emissions alone add $400,000 in costs under the European Union's Emissions Trading System.» The page does not say whether that is per voyage or per period, and it states the figure in a separate sentence from the one on longer routes, so the basis of the $400,000 and any link to rerouting are gaps. The pages hold no lane rate, charter rate or surcharge.\n\n*Analytical inference:* resting on the study's two estimates, the modelled yearly loss from delays, rerouting, insurance and trade disruption is larger than the added freight cost, and both are global annual figures, so they show the scale of exposure and not the price of any one lane. The sources name no competitor and no technology deployment, so this profile serves the cost, regulatory and client lenses only.\n\n## 2. Feasibility of Specific Operational Choices\n\nRerouting around the Cape of Good Hope is possible, and it is costly. UNCTAD reports: «In 2023, ship transits through these canals dropped by about half, forcing costly rerouting around Africa's Cape of Good Hope.» The scale shows in arrivals: «By mid-2024, Suez transits fell further, with ship capacity (tonnage) crossing the Gulf of Aden down 76% and tonnage transiting the Suez Canal cut by 70%. Cape of Good Hope arrivals surged 89%.» The capacity effect is stated too: «Longer routes raised global vessel ton-mile demand by 3% and container ship demand by 12%.» For Panama, «The Panama Canal disruption has increased sailing distances by 31% for affected routes.»\n\n*Analytical inference:* resting on those sentences, a carrier that avoids Suez or Panama can still move cargo by the longer route, but it uses more ship capacity to do so, since container ship demand rose 12% against 3% for ton-miles overall, and the sources do not state how long any extra voyage takes or what it adds to a quote. The IEA warns that tracking is imperfect: «GPS jamming, automatic identification system (AIS) spoofing, and vessels going dark are all reported issues in the region», so the flow counts for the Middle East chokepoints carry that caveat.\n\n## 4. Cross-Regional Strategic Implications\n\nWhich chokepoint matters depends on the trade lane. The study finds that «North America and the west coast of Latin America depend primarily on the Panama Canal, while the east coast of South America depends critically on the Cape of Good Hope». By value, «In value terms, around 20% of global maritime trade is shipped through the Taiwan Strait and Malacca Strait», and «Around 15% of maritime trade value transits the Suez Canal, Dover Strait, Strait of Gibraltar and Bab el-Mandeb Strait, mainly connecting Asia with Europe.» For energy, «around 21% of global petroleum consumption is shipped through the Strait of Hormuz». The IEA says of the current conflict: «The war in the Middle East has created the largest supply disruption in the history of the global oil market while also impeding critical flows of natural gas and other key commodities.»\n\n*Legal Confirmation Required:* whether and how the European Union's Emissions Trading System applies to a given voyage and who bears its cost is a question for counsel; the page states a cost figure and no rule.\n\n*Analytical inference:* resting on the UNCTAD sentences, two separate facts are held for the Far East-Europe lane, that longer routes boost emissions and that CO2 emissions alone add $400,000 for one vessel size under the European Union's Emissions Trading System; the page does not connect the two, so how much of that cost arises from sailing around the Cape is a gap, and so is any effect on an invoice.\n\n## 7. Pending Changes That Shift the Calculus\n\nA conflict is the live change. The IEA tool covers «since the start of the war in the region that began on 28 February 2026», and says «it will be updated regularly»; the IEA page records «Last updated 12 May 2026». The data behind it has gaps: IMF PortWatch states «Due to a disruption in incoming AIS data, we were unable to update the PortWatch data in December.», and the PortWatch dataset page the item points to says «This page may have been moved or deleted.» UNCTAD's projection, made in 2024, is a baseline: «The sector is projected to grow by 2% in 2024 and an average of 2.4% annually through 2029.» The 2024 year is past and the sources hold no outcome for it. It also calls for «Improved monitoring systems for early detection of chokepoint disruptions and faster rerouting».\n\n*Analytical inference:* resting on the IEA sentences, shipping flows through the three Middle East chokepoints have been measured since 28 February 2026, with the tool stating it will be updated regularly, so rerouting and cost conditions can change with the war, and no update after 12 May 2026 is held; the sources give no end date for the conflict and no schedule for the next IEA update, so both are gaps.\n\n- 28 February 2026: war in the Middle East begins, the start date of the IEA chokepoint shipping series.\n- 2029: end of UNCTAD's projection period of 2.4% average annual growth in maritime trade.\n\n## 8. Sources\n\n- IMF PortWatch dataset page: https://portwatch.imf.org/datasets/42132aa4e2fc4d41bdaf9a445f688931_0/about\n- Nature Communications, Systemic impacts of disruptions at maritime chokepoints: https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-025-65403-w\n- International Energy Agency, Middle East Maritime Chokepoints Shipping Monitor: https://www.iea.org/data-and-statistics/data-tools/middle-east-maritime-chokepoints-shipping-monitor\n- UNCTAD, Review of Maritime Transport 2024: https://unctad.org/publication/review-maritime-transport-2024\n",
