   "body": "## 1. Operational Cost Baseline for the Region\n\nRegion: this profile covers industrial electricity prices for European countries, for the United States and, through a world map, for 133 countries: «The data on the map are for 133 countries and were collected in 2025 Q3». It is a price comparison, not one national tariff.\n\nThe European Commission's energy department states the baseline as a multiple: «industrial gas and electricity prices, while lower than during the crisis, are still 2-4 times higher than in the EU’s main trading partners». The Commission report «was published in February 2025», so the multiple dates from then and is not a current rate. Trend direction: the same page says «Wholesale prices have stabilised on both electricity and gas markets since the energy crisis of 2021-2023» while retail energy prices are «still higher for households and enterprises than before 2021».\n\nThe International Energy Agency chart behind the comparison carries the note «Last updated 4 Mar 2026». It covers industries with «greater than 150 GWh of annual electricity consumption» for European countries, takes its data from Eurostat, and states «Electricity price compensation is included for countries that participate in EU-ETS». The source warns that «individual industries depending on their energy consumption levels and where they are located can face different prices», and that «The final electricity price for the United States is based off the final electricity for industry in Texas».\n\n*Analytical inference:* the chart covers the large-consumption band stated above and the sources give no consumption band for a freight site, so the chart's price is not stated to be a price a freight site would pay.\n\nGap: the captured text holds no price per kWh for any jurisdiction. The chart values sit in a graphic, and the page states «The data from this chart is not available for download». A tariff figure for a named jurisdiction has to come from a source that prints it.\n\n## 2. Feasibility of Specific Operational Choices\n\nVerdict: not established. The three captured pages are price and cost sources; none states a permit, grid-connection or equipment rule for any jurisdiction, so no operational choice can be called possible, restricted or prohibited from them. This section therefore carries a gap, not a verdict.\n\n*Analytical inference:* the only operational point these sources support is a price level, namely that European industrial electricity costs more than in the EU's main trading partners (Section 1), so any electricity-heavy operation sited in Europe starts from a higher energy price. The sources do not say which operations are affected or by how much.\n\n## 4. Cross-Regional Strategic Implications\n\nThe Commission states that «industrial gas and electricity prices, while lower than during the crisis, are still 2-4 times higher than in the EU’s main trading partners», so the comparison runs between Europe and other regions, not within Europe. *Analytical inference:* for an operator deciding where to place electricity-intensive activity across regions, this puts a structural energy-price gap on the European side of the comparison; the source names no freight activity and no share of operating cost, so the size of the effect on a freight site is not established here. The world map extends the price comparison: «The data on the map are for 133 countries and were collected in 2025 Q3», and «The prices are calculated using 1,000,000 kWh annual consumption», which makes its country values comparable with one another on that basis.\n\n## 7. Pending Changes That Shift the Calculus\n\nNo publicly announced change to these prices or to the way they are measured appears on the captured pages. The captured Commission page lists dashboards for energy prices and for energy costs of industry, and the IEA chart carries a last-updated note, but neither page announces a scheduled revision.\n\n- February 2025: the Commission report «was published in February 2025», stating that «industrial gas and electricity prices, while lower than during the crisis, are still 2-4 times higher than in the EU’s main trading partners».\n- 4 Mar 2026: the International Energy Agency's industrial electricity price chart carries the note «Last updated 4 Mar 2026»; this is the latest state of the series held here.\n\n## 8. Sources\n\n- European Commission, Directorate-General for Energy, Energy prices and costs in Europe (official publisher of the price assessment)\n- International Energy Agency, estimated final electricity price for large industrial customers in energy-intensive industries (intergovernmental data publisher)\n- GlobalPetrolPrices.com, industrial electricity prices world map (commercial price aggregator; its figures are a comparison dataset, not a tariff schedule)",
