   "body": "## 1. Operational Cost Baseline for the Region\n\nRegion: this profile covers New Mexico and, within it, the City of Albuquerque and Bernalillo County, where «Under New Mexico law , air quality regulation in the City of Albuquerque and Bernalillo County is the responsibility of local government rather than the state.» The rest of the state is regulated by the state environment department.\n\nThe cost figures the sources print are the fees of the state's Clean Transportation Fuel Program, which «establishes a statewide carbon intensity standard for transportation fuel that decreases annually»: «There is a one-time registration fee of $3,000 for CTFP credit and deficit generators and $500 for all other registered parties.» The sources name an annual fee as well but print no amount on the captured page. These are costs of the businesses that supply fuel, not a charge on a freight operator, so they sit upstream of any fuel price a haulier pays.\n\nHow a fuel supplier's compliance cost could reach a buyer is stated by the regulator in its own words. The New Mexico Environment Department says «CTFP is not a state tax or state fee and does not create or require a surcharge at the pump.», and also says «CTFP does not determine the price of credits or the cost of compliance: businesses and the credit market do.» It adds that «A party may even pass on more than what it actually costs for them to comply with programs like CTFP», which is the regulator's statement of what a supplier might do, not a measured price. The department also says that «any resulting price increase is projected to be minimal». No price per gallon or per litre is printed, so no figure for fuel cost is held here, and the trend direction is not stated.\n\nThe credit mechanism that creates the cost is stated plainly: companies whose fuel exceeds the standard must acquire credits, «while those with low-carbon fuel generate credits they can sell or trade». Retail sites are largely outside it: «The CTFP does not require retail petroleum gas or diesel stations to register as regulated parties, unless that retail station also imports fuel to the state of New Mexico.»\n\n*Analytical inference:* because the department says the credit price is set by businesses and the credit market, and prints no credit price, a freight buyer cannot read a diesel cost change off these pages; the first quote that shows a program-related fuel charge is the first evidence of its size.\n\n## 2. Feasibility of Specific Operational Choices\n\nChoice: buying new gasoline or diesel trucks for use in New Mexico. Verdict: possible. Reason: the department states that for medium- and heavy-duty trucks «it does not ban deliveries of new gasoline or diesel trucks», and that for cars and light trucks the standard «ACCII applies to vehicle manufacturers, not consumers.» *Operational implication:* the obligation on the zero-emission share of deliveries sits with the manufacturers, so a fleet operator is not barred from ordering conventional trucks; what changes is the mix of vehicles available to be delivered in the state.\n\nChoice: shifting a fleet to zero-emission trucks in the state. The sources state delivery shares, not charging, grid or tax-credit terms, so whether such trucks can be charged and operated on a given lane is not established by these pages; they point only to a separate tax credit page for clean cars and charging units.\n\n## 4. Cross-Regional Strategic Implications\n\nNew Mexico's fuel program links to other states' programs: the department lets a regulated party report with «Pathways approved under another state’s clean fuel program» as a temporary option. *Analytical inference:* the source names pathways approved under another state's clean fuel program as one of three temporary reporting options, so a supplier with such a pathway has a route to report here while it prepares a New Mexico pathway; the source does not say how many suppliers use it or what it costs.\n\nThe vehicle rules are the second link. The state adopted standards for new cars and trucks, and the Albuquerque and Bernalillo County board «the Albuquerque Bernalillo County Air Quality Control Board (AQCB) followed suit and adopted the nearly identical Clean Car Rule specific to the AQCB’s jurisdiction». *Analytical inference:* the city and county board adopted a rule the source calls nearly identical to the state rule, not identical; the pages do not say where the two differ.\n\n## 6. Client Conversation Talking Points\n\n- The state's fuel program sets «a statewide carbon intensity standard for transportation fuel that decreases annually», and the regulator says it does not create a surcharge at the pump; ask a fuel supplier in writing whether any program cost is passed on to the buyer, since the regulator says a supplier may do so.\n- The truck rule is a delivery share for manufacturers: «it does not ban deliveries of new gasoline or diesel trucks». Tell a client that ordering a conventional truck is not prohibited by it.\n- The truck delivery shares rise each model year, from the first row «2026-2027 2027 43% 15% 20% 15%» to «2031-2032 2032 82% 40% 60% 40%»; ask a client how its replacement cycle lines up with the model years in the table.\n\n## 7. Pending Changes That Shift the Calculus\n\nVehicle delivery shares start with Model Year 2027 and rise every year. The cars-and-light-trucks rule starts: «Starting with Model Year 2027, 43 percent of new light-duty vehicles delivered to the state must comply with the rule» and, «ending with Model Year 2032, the rule ensures that over 80% of new vehicles meet the standard». For trucks, the table lists shares by weight class for each model year; the first row reads «2026-2027 2027 43% 15% 20% 15%» and the 2032 row reads «2031-2032 2032 82% 40% 60% 40%» under the columns headed Class 2B-3, Class 4-8 and Class 7-8. The regulator states the endpoint: «ACT requires that 40-75% of new, on-road medium- and heavy-duty vehicles delivered for sale in the state be zero-emission vehicles by 2035.» It also states a reporting duty: «ACT also includes a one-time reporting requirement where entities will report information about their commercial medium- and heavy-duty vehicle (MHDV) usage.»\n\n*Legal Confirmation Required:* which fleets are the entities that report under the one-time reporting requirement, and whether a given fleet's trucks fall under the delivery shares, must be confirmed with counsel; the source does not define either.\n\n*Analytical inference:* reading the table columns in the order the source lists them, the zero-emission share of new truck deliveries rises each model year in every weight class shown, from 15%, 20% and 15% in the first row to 40%, 60% and 40% in the 2032 row; the source does not say which fleets are the entities that report.\n\nFuel program dates run on a quarterly clock: temporary fuel pathways may be used during «the first CTFP compliance period (April 1, 2026, to December 31, 2027)», with «plus an additional two-quarter grace period (until June 30, 2028) after NMED approval». The source stated, with a future date that has now passed, that the department «will begin accepting applications for New Mexico alternative fuel pathways as early as July 1, 2026». Applications for temporary pathways are due on a fixed schedule: «Organizations should submit applications for temporary pathways no later than 45 days after the end of the quarter for which the applicant seeks to report using the temporary pathway.»\n\nIn Albuquerque, a rule change is still in draft: «Public review draft of proposed amendment to 20.11.20 NMAC-Fugitive Dust Control is available for review and comment.» and «The AQP is soliciting feedback from the public on the draft changes prior to requesting a hearing before the Air Quality Control Board.» It concerns dust from land disturbance, not vehicles or fuel, and no hearing date is stated.\n\n- July 1, 2026: the source stated that the department «will begin accepting applications for New Mexico alternative fuel pathways as early as July 1, 2026»; this date has now passed and the page does not say when applications actually opened.\n- August 15, 2026: «the 45-day deadline would be August 15, 2026» for a temporary fuel pathway covering the first quarter of the program; this date has passed.\n- November 14, 2026: «the deadline would be November 14, 2026» for a temporary fuel pathway covering the second quarter of the program. \n- December 31, 2027: end of «the first CTFP compliance period (April 1, 2026, to December 31, 2027)».\n- June 30, 2028: end of the grace period, «plus an additional two-quarter grace period (until June 30, 2028) after NMED approval».\n\n## 8. Sources\n\n- New Mexico Environment Department, Climate Change Bureau: New Motor Vehicle Emissions Standards (Advanced Clean Cars II and Advanced Clean Trucks) page (state regulator)\n- New Mexico Environment Department, Climate Change Bureau: Clean Transportation Fuel Program page (state regulator)\n- City of Albuquerque Environmental Health Department, Air Quality Program: Regulation Development page (local regulator)\n- New Mexico Environment Department, Air Quality Bureau: Proposed Air Quality Regulations and Plans page (state regulator)",
