   "body": "## 1. Operational Cost Baseline for the Region\n\nRegion: the City of Los Angeles, within the State of California. The city's building efficiency ordinance is described by the city water and power utility this way: «Under the EBEWE ordinance, the City of Los Angeles requires that all buildings over 20,000 square feet (including municipal buildings over 7,500 square feet) conduct annual benchmarking of their energy and water use and then take action to reduce their consumption.»\n\nThe one dated cost change the captured pages print, on the building department's home page, is: «Effective June 9, 2026, the General Plan Maintenance Surcharge (GPMS), a.k.a. Planning General Plan Maintenance Surcharge, increases from 7% to 10%». The page names it a Planning surcharge; the captured text does not say which charges it is applied to, and it prints no dollar amount, so the change is a rise of three percentage points on an unstated base and not a cost per square foot. The state program has no filing cost: «The CEC Benchmarking Program does not charge a fee to submit reports.» The city program does carry fees, since it asks owners «to annually register their building (includes pay fees) and file a benchmark report of energy and water usage», and the captured pages print no amount for them.\n\nTime is a cost too. The utility's data hook-up for benchmarking is slow: «application processing may take 4-6 weeks», and the city guide advice is that «The Los Angeles Benchmarking Guide recommends submitting data request(s) no later than March 1st to allow sufficient processing time ahead of the annual June 1st deadline.»\n\n*Analytical inference:* a surcharge that rises from 7% to 10% adds three percentage points to whatever it is applied to; the pages give neither the base nor the building types it touches, so the effect on a given project is not established.\n\n## 2. Feasibility of Specific Operational Choices\n\nChoice: operating a privately owned existing building of 20,000 square feet or more in the City of Los Angeles. Verdict: possible, with an annual reporting duty on the owner. Reason: the ordinance's scope reaches buildings that are «Privately owned and are 20,000 square feet or more», and «the building owner shall annually submit to the Department an energy and water benchmarking report». The duty sits on the owner. *Operational implication:* the filing duty is the owner's; the source does not say who supplies the usage data for a leased building.\n\nThe ordinance protects that data: «Nothing in this chapter shall be construed to permit a building owner to use tenant utility usage data for purposes other than compliance with benchmarking report requirements.» It also draws a line around the building's own systems, leaving out «Systems or subsystems owned by a tenant or for which a tenant bears full maintenance responsibility, that are within the tenant's leased space and exclusively serve such leased space , and for which the tenant pays all the energy and water bills according to usage and demand as measured by a meter or sub-meter». Reporting covers more than energy: «The annual carbon dioxide equivalent emissions due to energy use for the building as estimated by ENERGY STAR».\n\nSome uses are outside the ordinance: «vehicle charging stations; utility pumping stations; treatment facilities ; sound stages ; structures primarily used for the production and post-production of motion pictures and television, and similar uses». A building used for film or television production is therefore among those the scope exempts, as the quoted list states; the exemption list names no other use.\n\n*Legal Confirmation Required:* whether a given leased facility is counted as a privately owned building of 20,000 square feet or more, and who files for it, is a question for the owner and counsel; the captured text does not answer it.\n\n## 4. Cross-Regional Strategic Implications\n\nCalifornia's state program covers fewer buildings: «Disclosable buildings are those with more than 50,000 square feet of gross floor area.» The Los Angeles ordinance reaches down to buildings of 20,000 square feet. *Analytical inference:* a building of more than 20,000 and not more than 50,000 square feet falls inside the city ordinance and outside the state definition of a disclosable building, so a site of that size in Los Angeles is subject to the city duty and not to the state definition, on the thresholds as stated.\n\nThe state page also lists «Information on local benchmarking and public disclosure programs for cities exempt from the state reporting requirement.» The captured pages do not say whether the City of Los Angeles is one of those cities, so whether a Los Angeles building reports to the state as well is not established here. The utility page says both the city ordinance and California's AB 802 are in play. The state calendar is annual: «Reporting is due every year by June 1 for buildings with no residential utility accounts», and «Owners of commercial buildings with more than 50,000 square feet and no residential utility accounts must report annually starting June 1, 2018.» The city utility's guide advises earlier data requests ahead of its own annual June deadline.\n\n## 7. Pending Changes That Shift the Calculus\n\nThe state energy commission has amendments on its docket: it held a «Workshop on Building Energy Benchmarking Regulatory Language Changes October 13, 2025», and its page lists a «Docket Log for 2025 Amendments (25-OIR-01)». The captured pages state no adoption date and no text of the changes, so the date and content of any new state duty are unknown.\n\n*Analytical inference:* because the content of the amendments is unknown, whether they alter who files, what is filed, the thresholds or the annual June 1 date is not established; the pages state only that amendments are on the energy commission's docket.\n\n- October 13, 2025: the state energy commission holds a workshop on regulatory language changes for building energy benchmarking.\n- June 9, 2026: «Effective June 9, 2026, the General Plan Maintenance Surcharge (GPMS), a.k.a. Planning General Plan Maintenance Surcharge, increases from 7% to 10%»; the effective date has passed.\n\n## 8. Sources\n\n- City of Los Angeles Department of Building and Safety (city building regulator): home page, with the surcharge notice\n- Los Angeles Department of Water and Power, Building Benchmarking page (city utility)\n- California Energy Commission, Building Energy Benchmarking Program page (state regulator)\n- City of Los Angeles open data catalog, Existing Buildings Energy and Water Efficiency Program dataset (city data publisher)\n- UpCodes, Los Angeles City Building Code, Chapter 97 Existing Buildings Energy and Water Efficiency Program (code publisher; text of the ordinance)\n- Note on the two captures: the data catalog says the program «was established in 2016» and the utility page says the city «established the Existing Buildings Energy and Water Efficiency (EBEWE) ordinance in 2017», so the two pages give different start years.",
