   "body": "## 1. Operational Cost Baseline for the Region\n\nRegion: the United Kingdom, with one regional layer for the North of England, where Transport for the North states «For TfN our focus is on reducing emissions from surface transport in the North».\n\nThe captured pages print no current price for diesel, electricity, labour, port handling or drayage in the United Kingdom, so no cost line is held here; they hold emissions shares and cost-parity assumptions instead. The parliamentary research office reports that «Surface transport is currently the highest greenhouse gas (GHG) emitting sector in the UK economy.» and that «In 2023, surface transport accounted for 24% of UK emissions: 59% of this came from cars, 19% HGVs, 18% vans». Among the vehicle types named, heavy goods vehicles are the second-largest source after cars and ahead of vans. For ships, «In 2023, shipping accounted for 3% of UK emissions, 11.2 MtCO 2 e.»\n\nOne cost-direction statement exists, and it is an assumption, not a price: the Climate Change Committee (CCC) assumes «new electric vehicles (EVs) assumed to reach price parity with fossil fuelled vehicles between 2026 and 2028». *Analytical inference:* if that assumption holds, a fleet buyer's purchase price for new electric vehicles stops carrying a premium over fossil-fuelled vehicles during that window; the source gives the assumption for new vehicles in general and states no heavy-vehicle price.\n\nGap: no cost figure for a UK freight operating input is in the captured text; the regional cost anchor has to come from a source that prints it.\n\n## 2. Feasibility of Specific Operational Choices\n\nChoice: electrifying heavy goods vehicles on UK roads. Verdict: not established by the sources; they state a projection and an open question. The CCC projection is that «By 2040, the CCC project that 63% of heavy good vehicles (HGVs) in the fleet need to be electric.», and the CCC acknowledges that «there is still some uncertainty regarding the exact make-up of technologies in the fleet that will meet requirements for specific long-distance journeys or for particularly heavy cargoes». The sources do not state a permit, grid or equipment rule, and they name no vehicle currently available for those journeys.\n\nChoice: shifting ships to low-carbon fuels. The sources say the pace is conditional: «the rate at which ships switch to these fuels is determined by the pace at which the technologies and fuels become available and cost effective». For seaports the position is weak: «No frameworks or monitoring tools are yet in place to assess progress towards net zero targets.»\n\n*Analytical inference:* for a road or sea carrier planning UK capacity, the sources support treating electrification as a trajectory the regulators and advisers expect, with the technology mix for heavy long-distance loads still open, not as a settled operating option today.\n\n## 4. Cross-Regional Strategic Implications\n\nThe UK national and regional layers set different horizons. Transport for the North states it is «committing to a regional near-zero carbon surface transport network by 2045», and the same page ties that to the national target: «2050 Net Zero target, it is highly likely that the transport sector, excluding aviation and shipping, will need to be almost completely decarbonised prior to 2050». *Analytical inference:* a regional body in the North of England sets a surface transport date (2045) ahead of the national net zero year (2050) named on the same page; the sources do not say what duties follow from either date.\n\nFor shipping the horizons are stated by the UK maritime strategy: «The 2025 UK Maritime decarbonisation strategy sets out goals to reduce greenhouse gas emissions by 30% by 2030, 80% by 2040 and to zero by 2050.» The same research office note projects: «Shipping emissions are projected to fall, relative to 2022 levels, by 62% to 5.4 MtCO2e by 2040», and says «low-carbon fuels and electricity will make up 54% of total shipping energy use in 2040».\n\nThe Innovate UK tracker connects these layers: «In 2021, the UK government published the UK Transport Decarbonisation Plan», and the tracker «48 Committments 9 Modes of transport 35 Policy papers 542 Policies». The tracker maps national commitments to regional and sub-regional policy, which is the cross-regional view this profile lacks in prices.\n\n## 7. Pending Changes That Shift the Calculus\n\nThe sources carry dated targets, not scheduled rule changes. Each is a goal or a projection by the body that states it. For surface transport, the CCC states that «surface transport emissions will need to have fallen by 86% from 2023 levels by 2040». For ships, «The 2025 UK Maritime decarbonisation strategy sets out goals to reduce greenhouse gas emissions by 30% by 2030, 80% by 2040 and to zero by 2050.» For ports, «In March 2025 the government launched an open call for evidence on challenges and opportunities for net zero ports.» The sources print no result of that call for evidence.\n\n*Analytical inference:* the dates 2030, 2040, 2045 and 2050 are targets and projections stated by advisers and strategy documents; none of the captured pages states a legal deadline on a freight operator.\n\n- 2030: UK Maritime decarbonisation strategy, first goal: 30% by 2030, as in «The 2025 UK Maritime decarbonisation strategy sets out goals to reduce greenhouse gas emissions by 30% by 2030, 80% by 2040 and to zero by 2050.»\n- 2040: CCC projections, «surface transport emissions will need to have fallen by 86% from 2023 levels by 2040», and «By 2040, the CCC project that 63% of heavy good vehicles (HGVs) in the fleet need to be electric.»\n- 2045: Transport for the North is «committing to a regional near-zero carbon surface transport network by 2045».\n- 2050: UK Maritime decarbonisation strategy, last goal: zero by 2050, as in «The 2025 UK Maritime decarbonisation strategy sets out goals to reduce greenhouse gas emissions by 30% by 2030, 80% by 2040 and to zero by 2050.»\n\n## 8. Sources\n\n- UK Parliament, Parliamentary Office of Science and Technology: Decarbonising surface transport, horizon scanning note (parliamentary research office; cites the Climate Change Committee)\n- Transport for the North, Decarbonisation page (sub-national transport body)\n- Innovate UK Business Connect, UK transport decarbonisation policy tracker (innovation agency)",
