   "body": "## 1. Operational Cost Baseline for the Region\n\nRegion: Japan, through the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure, Transport and Tourism (MLIT) Ports and Harbours Bureau, which states that «In October 2020, Japan declared its aim to \"reduce greenhouse gas emissions to net-zero by 2050». The captured pages concern Japanese ports and coastal areas; they name no other country.\n\nThe captured pages print no price for fuel, power, labour, port handling or drayage in Japan, so no operating cost figure is held here. What they print are the scale of the sector and the support for the build-out. Scale: «Ports are hubs of international supply chains, with over 99% of import and export cargo passing through them.» Concentration: «Many industries that account for about 60% of CO2 emissions are concentrated in ports and coastal areas.» Cost support: «Starting in FY2018, a subsidy program was established for the development of facilities needed to form LNG bunkering hubs in Japanese ports, taking advantage of the country's position as the world's largest importer of LNG.»\n\n*Analytical inference:* the Ministry ties its port programmes to supply chains that pass through ports; the source gives no tariff, surcharge or handling charge that would carry any cost to a shipper.\n\nGap: no sourced cost figure for a Japanese freight operating input is in the captured text; the regional cost anchor has to come from a source that prints it.\n\n## 2. Feasibility of Specific Operational Choices\n\nChoice: bunkering a ship with LNG in Japan. Verdict: possible in named areas, in preparation in another. Reason: «Japan's first Ship to Ship (STS) method of LNG bunkering was implemented in Ise and Mikawa Bays from October 2020.», under projects where «In June 2018, two projects were adopted: the LNG Bunkering Project in Ise and Mikawa Bays and the LNG Fuel Supply Project for Ships in Tokyo Bay under the STS Method.». For Tokyo Bay the position is not yet operating: «Also in Tokyo Bay, the construction and operation of LNG bunkering vessels are under preparation with the aim of putting the project into service at the earliest possible date.» The scheme widened again: «In FY2021, the LNG Bunkering Project in Kyushu and Setouchi Area based in Kitakyushu was adopted as the relevant subsidy program.»\n\nChoice: using a certified container terminal. Verdict: possible; a certification route exists: «the \"CNP Certification (Container Terminal)\" has been compiled and the system has been launched». The captured page does not name any terminal that holds the certification, and the criteria are on a separate portal.\n\n*Analytical inference:* the Ministry states that ship-to-ship bunkering has been implemented in Ise and Mikawa Bays from October 2020 and that Tokyo Bay is under preparation; the pages name no supplier, price, capacity or availability for a given sailing.\n\n## 4. Cross-Regional Strategic Implications\n\nThe Ministry states why ports are competing on decarbonisation: «it is necessary to developing competitive ports that are chosen by shippers and shipping companies». The shippers' side is also stated: «Shipper companies are working to decarbonize their entire supply chains, and ports are also required to respond to the needs of decarbonization.» *Analytical inference:* where a shipper's supply chain is being decarbonised, the choice among Japanese ports can follow the port's decarbonisation record, as the Ministry's own wording suggests; the source gives no share of cargo that moves this way.\n\nThe demand for LNG bunkering follows the shipping rules: the Ministry says that «Consequently, the number of vessels fueled by liquefied natural gas (LNG) is expected to increase.» That expectation is the Ministry's, with an international shipping rule behind it, and it is the reason given for building bunkering hubs at several ports.\n\nA road logistics trial is the one operating result in the pages. The Ministry of Economy, Trade and Industry (METI) and MLIT chemicals working group ran a trial: «to carry out a demonstration test over September–December 2024», with the result that «The on-site trial in particular found striking results, including a 20 percentage point improvement in truck fill rate and a 28 percent reduction in CO2 emissions.» *Analytical inference:* this is a trial result between named chemical complexes, from a news write-up, and not a measured national figure; it shows that load sharing between regions can raise truck fill, and the source does not extend the result beyond the chemical sector.\n\n## 7. Pending Changes That Shift the Calculus\n\nThree streams run through the pages, each with its own date or open end. First, Tokyo Bay: «Also in Tokyo Bay, the construction and operation of LNG bunkering vessels are under preparation with the aim of putting the project into service at the earliest possible date.» No date is stated. Second, offshore wind in ports, which will need ports that can handle heavy equipment: the Ministry says the Offshore Wind Industry Vision indicated «projects of 30 to 45 million kW would be formed by 2040», and that a base port system «allows long-terms (up to 30 years) and stable leasing of wharves at base ports and harbors to power companies». Third, the national target: «In October 2020, Japan declared its aim to \"reduce greenhouse gas emissions to net-zero by 2050».\n\n*Analytical inference:* these dates are goals and plans stated by the Ministry, not legal deadlines on a freight operator; the first two shape which ports have fuel and heavy-lift capacity, the third frames the policy direction for all ports.\n\n- October 2020: «Japan's first Ship to Ship (STS) method of LNG bunkering was implemented in Ise and Mikawa Bays from October 2020.»\n- 2040: offshore wind goal, «projects of 30 to 45 million kW would be formed by 2040».\n- 2050: Japan's declared aim, «In October 2020, Japan declared its aim to \"reduce greenhouse gas emissions to net-zero by 2050».\n\n## 8. Sources\n\n- Ministry of Land, Infrastructure, Transport and Tourism (MLIT), Ports and Harbours Bureau: 2.4 Carbon Neutral Port (CNP) (national transport ministry)\n- AlchemPro news desk (Fibre2Fashion), report on the METI and MLIT chemicals working group joint logistics trial (trade news write-up of a government-backed trial)",
