   "body": "## 1. Operational Cost Baseline for the Region\n\nRegion: Nova Scotia, a province of Canada, whose government says «Nova Scotia has legislated ambitious climate goals in the Environmental Goals and Climate Change Reduction Act». The Act is described as one that «It contains 28 goals that will reduce greenhouse gas emissions, grow the green and circular economies».\n\nThe captured pages print no price for electricity, fuel, labour, port handling or drayage in Nova Scotia, and no carbon price. They do say where the emissions sit: «Most greenhouse gas emissions emitted in Nova Scotia come from the use of fossil fuels for generating electricity, transporting people and goods, and heating homes and buildings.» They also say a pricing system exists: the Environment Act «was amended in November 2022 to create» an output-based pricing system, «to put a price on greenhouse gas emissions from large emitters». The pages print no price level under that system, so no cost line is held here.\n\n*Analytical inference:* because the province states that fossil fuel use for transporting goods is one of the main sources of its emissions, the targets below bear on goods movement, but the pages state no charge, surcharge or fuel standard that falls on a freight operator.\n\nGap: no sourced cost figure for a Nova Scotia freight operating input is in the captured text; the regional cost anchor has to come from a source that prints it.\n\n## 2. Feasibility of Specific Operational Choices\n\nChoice: relying on grid electricity for electrified freight equipment in Nova Scotia. Verdict: not established by the sources. The federal and provincial governments cap the electricity sector's emissions: the equivalency agreement records «mandatory greenhouse gas emissions limits for the electricity sector in Nova Scotia for the years 2025 to 2029», namely «for the calendar year 2025, not greater than 6 Mt of CO 2 e» and «for the calendar years 2026 to 2029, not greater than 21.5 Mt of CO 2 e cumulative total». The pages state no grid-connection rule, charging tariff or permit condition for any operator.\n\n*Analytical inference:* the caps limit the greenhouse gas emissions of electricity generation in the province, not the price or the supply of power to a freight site; whether a given electrified fleet can be charged on a given lane is not answered by these pages.\n\n## 4. Cross-Regional Strategic Implications\n\nNova Scotia's electricity rules link to the federal government's. The agreement states that «Provisions that are in force by or under the laws of Nova Scotia, and in particular the Environment Act and the Greenhouse Gas Emissions Regulations are equivalent to the provisions of the CEPA and the Coal-fired Electricity Regulations», and that «The penalty and enforcement provisions of the Environment Act are equivalent to the penalty and enforcement provisions in the CEPA.» *Analytical inference:* the agreement is between the two governments and concerns «the control of greenhouse gas emissions from electricity producers in Nova Scotia»; it binds the electricity producers' regime and states no duty on a freight operator, and the pages say nothing about other provinces.\n\nIf the arrangement ends early, the federal rules take its place: «Canada will ensure that the Coal-fired Electricity Regulations start applying in Nova Scotia with as little disruption as possible in the circumstances». The agreement allows early exit: «This agreement may be terminated earlier by either party giving the other at least three months’ notice.»\n\n## 7. Pending Changes That Shift the Calculus\n\nTwo layers of dates apply, both goals set in law by the province and a fixed-term federal arrangement. Targets: the Act sets «reduce greenhouse gas emissions by 53% below 2005 levels by 2030» and to «achieve net-zero greenhouse gas emissions by 2050». The government's plan «outlines how we will reach our 2030 target and put us on a pathway to net-zero greenhouse gas emissions». Reporting: «The Act requires the Minister of Environment and Climate Change to report annually to the House of Assembly on the progress made towards the goals.» The list of reports includes the «2025 Environmental Goals and Climate Change Reduction Act and Climate Change Plan – Progress Report». Agreement: the equivalency agreement covers «mandatory greenhouse gas emissions limits for the electricity sector in Nova Scotia for the years 2025 to 2029», and a reassessment can follow new rules: «either Party can undertake a reassessment of the determination of equivalency between the federal and provincial regimes and conclude that reassessment within 24 months of the promulgation of the regulatory instrument».\n\n*Analytical inference:* these are targets and reporting duties on the province and federal government; none of the captured pages states a deadline or charge on a freight operator, so the effect on freight arrives, if at all, through rules not yet in the pages.\n\n- 2030: provincial target, «reduce greenhouse gas emissions by 53% below 2005 levels by 2030».\n- 2050: provincial target, to «achieve net-zero greenhouse gas emissions by 2050».\n- 2029: last year of the electricity-sector limits, «mandatory greenhouse gas emissions limits for the electricity sector in Nova Scotia for the years 2025 to 2029».\n\n## 8. Sources\n\n- Government of Nova Scotia, Department of Environment and Climate Change (provincial regulator): department page and Environmental Goals and Climate Change Reduction Act progress reporting page\n- Government of Nova Scotia, Climate Change Nova Scotia: What Nova Scotia is Doing (provincial government)\n- Government of Canada, Canadian Environmental Protection Act Registry: Canada-Nova Scotia equivalency agreement regarding greenhouse gas emissions from electricity producers, 2025 (federal government)",
