   "body": "## 1. Operational Cost Baseline for the Region\n\nRegion: the United States, at federal level, as described by the Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) and a think-tank policy summary. The EPA states that «transportation accounted for the largest portion (28%) of total U.S. GHG emissions in 2022».\n\nThe captured pages print no price for diesel, electricity, labour, port handling or drayage in the United States, so no operating cost line is held here. What they print is the emissions baseline of the transport sector. Share of the whole economy: «In 2022, direct and indirect greenhouse gas emissions from transportation accounted for 29% of total U.S. greenhouse gas emissions». Where it comes from: «The largest sources of transportation greenhouse gas emissions in 2022 were light-duty trucks, which include sport utility vehicles, pickup trucks, and minivans (37%); medium- and heavy-duty trucks (23%); passenger cars (20%); commercial aircraft (7%); other aircraft (2%); pipelines (4%); ships and boats (3%); and rail (2%).». Medium- and heavy-duty trucks are the second-largest source after light-duty trucks. Trend: «From 1990 to 2022, total transportation emissions from fossil fuel combustion increased by 19%. From 2021 to 2022, emissions decreased by less than 1%.»\n\nAnother summary, for 2020, gives a different grouping: «Medium- and heavy-duty vehicles, which include tractor-trailers, large pickups and vans, delivery trucks, buses, and garbage trucks, produce about 26 percent of transportation emissions.» The two pages use different years and different vehicle groupings, so the 23% and the 26 percent are not a trend.\n\nCold chain is named: «In addition, hydrofluorocarbon (HFC) emissions also occur from the transportation sector. These emissions result from the use of mobile air conditioners and refrigerated transport.»\n\nGap: no sourced cost figure for a United States freight operating input is in the captured text; the regional cost anchor has to come from a source that prints it.\n\n## 2. Feasibility of Specific Operational Choices\n\nChoice: switching a freight fleet's fuel or practice in the United States. Verdict: not established by the sources as a permit or rule question; the EPA lists options and states no prohibition. The EPA table names fuel switching, efficiency, operating practices and travel demand as ways to reduce transport emissions, and points freight to a programme: «Learn about how the freight transportation industry can reduce emissions through EPA's SmartWay Program». One operating practice is listed in the table: «Reducing engine-idling.»\n\n*Analytical inference:* these are opportunities listed by the agency, not requirements on an operator; the pages state no vehicle, fuel or equipment that is prohibited or restricted for a freight fleet, and no price for any of the options.\n\n## 4. Cross-Regional Strategic Implications\n\nVehicle rules are not uniform across the country. The summary states: «California is the only state allowed to set its own air emissions standards for motor vehicles.» and «Seventeen states and the District of Columbia, making up about 40 percent of U.S. auto sales, currently follow at least some of California». *Analytical inference:* a fleet that operates in a state following California's standards and in one that does not can face different vehicle rules in each; the page states this for new motor vehicle standards generally and gives no heavy-truck detail, and the figure of states is as stated by the summary, which is not dated on the captured page.\n\n## 7. Pending Changes That Shift the Calculus\n\nThe federal rules named in the pages were set mostly before this profile. For heavy trucks, the summary states: «EPA also released new standards limiting nitrogen oxide (NOx) emissions from heavy-duty trucks in December 2022.» and «It also directs the agencies to issue standards for medium- and heavy-duty trucks for model years 2027 through 2030, which were finalized in December 2022.» Both dates are past. The captured pages state the standards as finalized and give no later change.\n\nThe federal policy goal is non-binding: «In August 2021, President Biden signed Executive Order 14037». «The order sets a non-binding target of making 50 percent of all new passenger car and light truck sales ZEVs in 2030.» The same summary says the order directs agencies to finalise passenger vehicle standards, «to be finalized no later than July 2024»; that date has passed and the captured pages do not state whether the standards were finalized or whether the order remains in force.\n\n*Legal Confirmation Required:* whether Executive Order 14037 and the standards it directed are still in force, and which rules now govern medium- and heavy-duty trucks, must be confirmed with counsel; the captured pages predate any later change and do not answer it.\n\n- December 2022: the EPA's heavy-duty truck nitrogen oxide standards and the medium- and heavy-duty standards for model years 2027 through 2030 are released and finalized, as the summary states.\n- July 2024: date by which standards for passenger cars and light trucks were to be finalized under the order; the date has passed.\n- 2030: the order's non-binding target for zero-emission vehicle sales; no freight target is stated.\n\n## 8. Sources\n\n- U.S. Environmental Protection Agency (EPA): transportation sector emissions, sources of greenhouse gas emissions and Fast Facts on Transportation Greenhouse Gas Emissions pages (federal environmental regulator)\n- U.S. Department of Transportation, Bureau of Transportation Statistics: Freight Facts and Figures, domestic freight greenhouse gas emissions page (federal statistics agency; the captured page carries navigation and a table title only)\n- Center for Climate and Energy Solutions (C2ES): Regulating transportation sector carbon emissions (non-profit policy think-tank; a policy summary and not a regulator)",
